#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82fdd23c-cc42-585e-88f4-5b182b40e621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398516} {:answer/id #uuid "23aa505d-ac0a-5049-b866-d1c5cc38bc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398517} {:answer/id #uuid "e4493f5f-d9a0-53f7-a4c1-2310218fdc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398518} {:answer/id #uuid "8e689bac-8fa3-596f-82fa-c4985e2215d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398519} {:answer/id #uuid "8b17b653-bdb9-591a-a066-41676556f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5u-6v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b17b653-bdb9-591a-a066-41676556f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5u-6v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398520} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15555d0-ef8f-523d-8686-3e2c75b76cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eec4b66c-4af6-5b13-942b-337cb7b8732a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161357" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,u+(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,3u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,3u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-2)+(2u-v)\\cdot (2)+(3u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u-2v+4u-2v+3u-2v \\\\  & =5u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-6v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(5u-6v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c22e69-9068-5d72-8593-ce78861036f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398523} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4db3f6-9c97-526b-8cb2-065c8f5de466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398524} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2e8a80-65c9-5d70-9b0e-0e801e7e718d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u+v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398525} {:answer/id #uuid "9f182d4d-0129-52ff-90b2-56e5acb487b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u-v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398526} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e4fd3a-37bb-5bcd-85c6-185c9a1d61c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+uv-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c22e69-9068-5d72-8593-ce78861036f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398523} :answer-field/id #uuid "396da9a4-de3b-5bd7-8b5e-dfc780e10bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5e1155b-c484-5f31-a815-946d2d63d6df" :question/math-academy-id "q-161359" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)v\\cdot (-1)+(u-v)u\\cdot (1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-uv+{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}-uv+uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a60a0ff-d58d-574d-ad7b-aa68f48f45e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398564} {:answer/id #uuid "caf3aed4-56d3-57fb-9c54-0b9379ad0474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398565} {:answer/id #uuid "5e393df8-940d-5225-8bcc-4aba9a085593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398566} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebc6e76-bfea-5200-aea9-f978a3678ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398567} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568} :answer-field/id #uuid "196954bd-17a6-525e-a3ea-36557f290435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d14173-8029-50a6-8f00-d7853c057071" :question/math-academy-id "q-162581" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc53f04c-d5bb-59dd-b389-8e0eefab0229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398571} {:answer/id #uuid "af12ea26-8299-5100-8312-7691bb7b67f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398572} {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} {:answer/id #uuid "221c39d4-97a7-58e1-af0f-bd5bd145ae18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398574} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84079a-9c1c-54c9-bc3c-5fd874d6a1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b4c4f1b-ebdf-554c-9f5f-c8043f8e6124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20faa8ac-7475-55a0-94fb-35eb1571259e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162583" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,6x-z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146d3b28-8e29-5eb2-9b1c-13a2a90ec385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399498} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d5787b-b5bd-5b27-a033-31a126fb604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399499} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd95806-d79f-5932-8502-3d798b511be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7286f56-415b-5975-9965-cac2f25bbe61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399501} {:answer/id #uuid "4df757e2-103b-56a4-a77e-0738b69bd499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4d5787b-b5bd-5b27-a033-31a126fb604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399499} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f37ecc-6d85-5118-a9aa-9f9f01af2578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656ebe17-b886-53ff-b069-a7253f396457" :question/math-academy-id "q-106049" :question/problem "![](images/21/2135f70f6703878d5f82d3524c890af08e194d7fdfbe92e97a5305d142f73779.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f37153f-6b80-5708-b6a4-edebf7c3a823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399505} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b71958-0b7a-58d5-aa67-e69e50d38e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399506} {:answer/id #uuid "e501da01-42b8-58ee-9609-f73020f69f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399507} {:answer/id #uuid "08a3ceb4-1e5e-5d85-b566-0b5a554e82e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399508} {:answer/id #uuid "08282cf0-c65d-5a51-98dd-3a937c9e9dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08a3ceb4-1e5e-5d85-b566-0b5a554e82e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399508} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b61b62a-5e5d-5935-a620-97cbe92e0582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45d1161-cc6e-56e5-89da-543c3e758d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106051" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6379c3-3e1c-5a08-ae54-d99f43489aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400067} {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9efa8-1c48-5867-a1e9-734559b5450d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400069} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a9aab-60c9-5ec0-8bc2-0588e38d3687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e29ac-c571-528d-b8c7-bb6ee10f19df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e41e50-ea1d-54b4-8438-62f4c2b9fa61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c848ce1a-65b8-563c-9fec-80599c592bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -3,\\,y\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400116} {:answer/id #uuid "7f194208-efc2-515c-a071-37d890d6515f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400117} {:answer/id #uuid "2b898a9d-7842-5e4a-9365-22c2934152ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400118} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1c0d33-169a-5055-a64e-c6a8e8656c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400119} {:answer/id #uuid "658dd7e1-62d5-534e-8c6f-6d03db279945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3,\\,y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b898a9d-7842-5e4a-9365-22c2934152ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400118} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb683db-68cc-56c9-829b-45a54bf4f29a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3e9c6f6-566c-5162-9fe9-4d86fd16d1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123769" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3-t,\\,\\,\\,y=t+2,\\,t\\ge 1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $3-t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge 1.$ Consequently, $x=3-t\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $t+2\\ge 3$ for all $t\\ge 1.$ Consequently, $y=t+2\\ge 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le 2$ and $y\\ge 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "befcfe4a-abd3-5984-a1a3-1a1bf2b0cd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400123} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee13255-e4e9-52e6-b06e-d0aeddb3bb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400124} {:answer/id #uuid "c3236814-4c23-5fc9-a82d-b9432370c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400125} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd316cc-7219-5b56-b38c-fa0c32ea3d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400126} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127} :answer-field/id #uuid "806b0a66-23a8-530b-9fd2-35a679c8dd3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42535d46-cb00-5afc-82ab-e62c0ea06521" :question/math-academy-id "q-123773" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\ge -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -1 \\\\ -1 & \\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1-1 & \\le 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+1 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le 1+2 \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64998bfc-bfc7-5d3e-8ee6-300d3a38797d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-4\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400130} {:answer/id #uuid "42492b12-0052-541d-8cee-a36023e92984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400131} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4ef62b-01d3-5f37-819a-bf2a72b19db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400132} {:answer/id #uuid "79eb9da7-f01c-5dfb-a236-47a585231977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400133} {:answer/id #uuid "617de8a7-03f5-5305-95c8-3f2f2a0cf8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79eb9da7-f01c-5dfb-a236-47a585231977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6815f3-6a4d-5946-a00b-a698526a94b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df6cb91b-a536-5526-9dd6-80b470cc8a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-123775" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $2\\le y\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f43fbb-95b7-541f-8b6c-884210699c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400752} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6022ff-41cc-5e6f-94c9-3fc31db2cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,14,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400753} {:answer/id #uuid "45ff73b4-62a8-5ce3-88c9-d43c398f4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-10,2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400754} {:answer/id #uuid "904fd9b0-d482-5093-afb0-55078a667399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-14,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400755} {:answer/id #uuid "f884e2c3-8624-5ca1-87bf-175fa491b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f43fbb-95b7-541f-8b6c-884210699c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-2,30⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400752} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f171d5e-1ede-583a-b2ab-a54f8c858c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ea4789-04ff-53b9-83f0-42f591cf2261" :question/math-academy-id "q-1393" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨4,5,-1⟩$ and $v=⟨-6,0,2⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-6))j+(4\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-6))k \\\\  & =(10-0)i-(8-6)j+(0-(-30))k \\\\  & =10i-2j+30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60199c4-dc25-5568-af40-de39cd26589d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401071} {:answer/id #uuid "41c812bd-cb3b-5fc5-aef0-fb585602e943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401072} {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc02f7-9666-528e-9191-ebdcde2135fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401074} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea8ed64-c406-5164-b57d-847952b47323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc7c1c-c03b-5382-bd6b-5a9beec491d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9e0331-ffe6-5489-b436-faf1f0bea590" :question/math-academy-id "q-15469" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}+1,\\,y={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4t({t}^{2}+1)}{6{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({t}^{2}+1)}{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2((1)^{2}+1)}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6ce404-ebcc-51cc-943b-4e4e79eb614d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2R$ and $-2P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401242} {:answer/id #uuid "44cf614b-483e-57c6-b48c-bdea43bdf8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401243} {:answer/id #uuid "920e8870-c9e3-5c16-bd67-bc6987066749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401244} {:answer/id #uuid "7051dd66-a8a1-5bd2-a47a-cbbfa2e9ad6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401245} {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401246} :answer-field/id #uuid "d028cbae-7e56-54b5-84a6-c407d88b99d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75438633-2fed-5dc6-8f93-bd3571d872a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161455" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=2x{z}^{3},$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,2x{z}^{3},z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,2{z}^{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,6x{z}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2{z}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6x{z}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨2{z}^{3},\\,-1,\\,6x{z}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,{z}^{3}+Q-6R\\,x{z}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4fc5fe-cb91-578b-8dbf-4a5ffd8c3fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401249} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9077fe-1593-50c2-8f89-3d6b0dcabdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401250} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8485ee-e4b0-5c7e-96b8-4bdcc10e86bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401251} {:answer/id #uuid "09583e22-9aba-582a-80e6-e8808ccbeebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401252} {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee6b27a-bcd5-52e8-9493-e839011ba707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "417f7e3b-659c-5f7d-913c-decf384148f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161457" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffab214-a531-5f16-9548-4aba64a14e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂z$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401256} {:answer/id #uuid "27be7768-bf01-5a25-8f76-a7b3aa6827e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401257} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b36ca-8d50-5d13-b2d4-01dff2d1189e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401258} {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} {:answer/id #uuid "401f8a2b-7596-5052-8ec8-449369280a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585e533-2d5e-5fa5-b53e-85ce1c70e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2128236-dd4e-5cec-8d28-c07554df0f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161463" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab621f-8cde-5ff7-83da-d05fc13356ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401263} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1077bb-f87e-5cca-9aef-16e4fb33aef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401264} {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} {:answer/id #uuid "6170ad11-316c-53bb-b473-67e1f8f56f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401266} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc272-4ba4-53bf-8e7e-06633eea609a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d473e4-fb62-570e-a504-252ea46fe0d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682d2ef7-9cff-5a52-921d-70cd49bbb080" :question/math-academy-id "q-161476" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{y-x}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{x-y}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401845} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886ea0dd-2f3b-5230-a912-ca494bbb228c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401855} {:answer/id #uuid "1b58ab2c-a7ae-5296-9ea9-aaaf06b0d86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401856} {:answer/id #uuid "3480faea-4f30-5410-8c55-291a77967982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401857} {:answer/id #uuid "1933a3b0-751e-5adc-a9a1-355a8352674b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401858} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401859} :answer-field/id #uuid "532bfa22-570e-5521-a58b-09bf6df9181b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c14e172-0d42-58ef-86b2-05fb93de9caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-20990" :question/problem "![](images/58/5855e92b04ffbf8447d32c7edb190cd16046a13e8808e6ffaade1f59e414c869.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+2t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+2}{2{e}^{t}(t+1)}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9abfdb74-a53a-5872-9e51-bd0aa07bb10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473082} {:answer/id #uuid "9e287248-2f2b-5be8-9e40-4ece19f43e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e287248-2f2b-5be8-9e40-4ece19f43e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473083} :answer-field/id #uuid "87236ea7-a2fc-5398-9f98-8b0dec672075" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72057f6-fbf3-5494-8883-b63e2381aab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473084} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fe42c2-35b2-5914-abe8-4b65467d7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72057f6-fbf3-5494-8883-b63e2381aab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473084} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6a83fd-694a-5132-a8a6-4888b5faa104" :answer-field/key "$c=-1$ gives a parabola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a858058d-dbdf-59ef-a7db-6b095c446be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473086} {:answer/id #uuid "1cefc54e-0efc-53ef-8cc1-fff5488204b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cefc54e-0efc-53ef-8cc1-fff5488204b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473087} :answer-field/id #uuid "29967360-3ab9-5db6-88fc-1504799fabb3" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb8b2d04-67d4-51d3-8ebf-71fc804b1a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-35527" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy=\\pm 1,$ which are hyperbolas.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\nSo, only statements “$c=1$ gives a hyperbola” and “$c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b7074a-75b7-5dfa-ba96-9067b0b0c2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403274} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca59391-4b16-5de2-b31a-e434042882de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403275} {:answer/id #uuid "b7da2d97-ee19-5b91-aae1-d3e1ef8e41cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403276} {:answer/id #uuid "c0595185-9dbe-5e61-833d-f322b3bc5d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403277} {:answer/id #uuid "efa241d0-aa74-5ee0-84e5-879e44d2f438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0595185-9dbe-5e61-833d-f322b3bc5d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403277} :answer-field/id #uuid "cade3433-2a7c-573e-ae14-9f4f0ad1cbde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef78af33-68fa-5c96-9f7c-05772b13cffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-6812" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=({t}^{3}-1){e}^{t},\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{({0}^{3}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-1){e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{(-1)\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc882f2-3f87-5d1b-873e-989959c29c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403281} {:answer/id #uuid "571f5f41-efb0-58e5-a46f-8c213ca0a973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403282} {:answer/id #uuid "73cec9e6-12bd-5c23-9094-78dadbc4e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403283} {:answer/id #uuid "a99bf228-d02b-5fa9-b6b7-158b7cd89312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403284} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb8bf5d-1f44-5559-a2f8-15d082ac4b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a99bf228-d02b-5fa9-b6b7-158b7cd89312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403284} :answer-field/id #uuid "81664d19-fcb9-599f-8db6-ec3e35d93bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b50faac3-57b3-5534-a1d3-8f76087cb51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6836" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd57751-d597-520b-a7e4-71f3186dd313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403309} {:answer/id #uuid "d2572f8b-0d83-5b65-9e95-e308752dfc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403310} {:answer/id #uuid "bed4103a-4b2a-5d61-a667-474d6dd5518f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403311} {:answer/id #uuid "7251e72d-ced9-526a-b908-ef4dfa62c0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403312} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1d58d4-3ea6-5b93-9ea1-e559f66e05b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1d58d4-3ea6-5b93-9ea1-e559f66e05b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403313} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8576c6-bfec-538d-b766-7a18236f2219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adc81ace-55ff-5a6f-a052-59cb6a0b2712" :question/math-academy-id "q-6877" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be7179d0-b303-5a2e-b56b-b3b257addb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403323} {:answer/id #uuid "cec3ba52-e1e4-5a4f-8355-2de1d95da95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403324} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a7db8-9a21-5d87-8b40-5eaa6730b4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403325} {:answer/id #uuid "05fa8f18-559c-511f-8fa5-e7bd79e27bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403326} {:answer/id #uuid "b5524ee3-4819-53b5-b113-cac820959124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be7179d0-b303-5a2e-b56b-b3b257addb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403323} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab05f15b-9c0b-54cc-bbed-6848d9cdd623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68b3cf46-70bc-587e-8a8a-2aa59902205d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6893" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d90adc7e7badb9c43763febb960b1a8a2b13884c4ce913110264d5c1998ac7b.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $12$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(12,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=12.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9f73d83-c051-5dff-95b7-3c8c97c9edbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403677} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14150b-a7fa-5e1f-8e26-8118219d8796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0,\\,y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403678} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0317e7-34d8-5e8a-a56c-876d04157a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403679} {:answer/id #uuid "0117c445-1ef3-5ae6-aae5-46c6b2367762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4,\\,y\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403680} {:answer/id #uuid "a9339c84-b774-56f5-95e7-f320ba73550e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9339c84-b774-56f5-95e7-f320ba73550e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403681} :answer-field/id #uuid "966a4be9-e62e-532a-b5f4-b29c14204104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc2dccb-24dd-5003-b2b4-73d21d258e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73617" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\ge -1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $1-t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge -1.$ Consequently, $x=1-t\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{2}-4\\ge -4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le 2$ and $y\\ge -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5eb255d-3cfa-5ae7-b5a0-ab9f9f6b8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403824} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb81c4-43fa-5409-88e4-4f5ef499dd13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403825} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} {:answer/id #uuid "9555e468-7e78-5b3e-afb9-8e76c044cd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403827} {:answer/id #uuid "13841314-a76c-5620-906a-428ce704c6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc1b61e-03fa-5261-ae12-a9f784057a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75288" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21e75e6-c690-5426-9022-4e65c4cd597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405038} {:answer/id #uuid "858df234-13ff-5820-8cad-fd7b874bd17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405039} {:answer/id #uuid "132a6cae-f594-52cb-8c0a-5014dac8b218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} {:answer/id #uuid "289d6efd-e337-519a-9f4f-3e9ef2eb9804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041} {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f53d24-ffcb-51fb-8ef8-920f533f7ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" :question/math-academy-id "q-126539" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017f8f24-8529-55cc-a1a8-41dc999d12f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0e048-b5e5-51cc-8ee4-8a54d22a60bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405046} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65de8-c1f0-596d-875a-4aa5f454bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047} {:answer/id #uuid "0592c862-0b50-54dc-acf8-8691c79146ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405048} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a141bb-7515-52e5-991a-6d1cce5fd112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405052} {:answer/id #uuid "15639b84-a5cd-5c57-999c-5eaa4da55cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405053} {:answer/id #uuid "153703e1-22d9-5301-b881-b997b52a3787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405054} {:answer/id #uuid "08da98f7-5e83-58f1-9af7-d12e18b8d86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405055} {:answer/id #uuid "bd69317b-3202-5a07-9b1f-77c79a7cbb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a141bb-7515-52e5-991a-6d1cce5fd112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405052} :answer-field/id #uuid "d59276d0-6151-587a-b33c-8bc27d781877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0f4b84a-3d7c-5e64-bd13-2b1a225fb6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126748" :question/problem "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755d214e-8d2a-5a21-b9cf-f0df0ddc65e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437406} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfd0671-10c2-5e45-a69d-18a97c5bcb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "755d214e-8d2a-5a21-b9cf-f0df0ddc65e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437406} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fca85a-8f3f-5d04-ba2b-523fc3575ba4" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d7a93e-05d3-5b4e-a10d-309d06525f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437408} {:answer/id #uuid "cee06875-116c-5488-b531-455a557b12b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7a93e-05d3-5b4e-a10d-309d06525f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437408} :answer-field/id #uuid "77753f43-8be4-5002-8a52-0b36dbebd616" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0919152f-4318-5ee5-ab9e-853f1a97e081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437410} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd97fc8-8c06-56b4-a75e-62d1a96d9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd97fc8-8c06-56b4-a75e-62d1a96d9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437411} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9e3e64-ecdb-5c87-af06-d417e36b04d6" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98360c07-0eb0-5614-8178-f21e14480fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437412} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7d8c5d-3f07-50af-a145-77ac2e6077ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98360c07-0eb0-5614-8178-f21e14480fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437412} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0366b6-425d-5c79-b027-f2d81a491b79" :answer-field/key "$F$ is solenoidal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2847820b-e13e-563d-bfbb-bab640514e5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105572" :question/problem "![](images/58/584016c88e5916984e181118d52caab95dba1fd0d861e0fd248a4e023d1b07da.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ only."}]}
